{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"code","value":"function createAsyncThrottler<TFn, TSelected>(\n   fn,\n   options,\nselector?): SolidAsyncThrottler<TFn, TSelected>;","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Defined in: "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/pacer/blob/main/packages/solid-pacer/src/async-throttler/createAsyncThrottler.ts#L180","children":[{"type":"text","value":"async-throttler/createAsyncThrottler.ts:180"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A low-level Solid hook that creates an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncThrottler"},{"type":"text","value":" instance to limit how often an async function can execute."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This hook is designed to be flexible and state-management agnostic - it simply returns a throttler instance that\nyou can integrate with any state management solution (createSignal, etc)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async throttling ensures an async function executes at most once within a specified time window,\nregardless of how many times it is called. This is useful for rate-limiting expensive API calls,\ndatabase operations, or other async tasks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unlike the non-async Throttler, this async version supports returning values from the throttled function,\nmaking it ideal for API calls and other async operations where you want the result of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maybeExecute"},{"type":"text","value":" call\ninstead of setting the result on a state variable from within the throttled function."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error Handling:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" handler is provided, it will be called with the error and throttler instance"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" is true (default when no onError handler is provided), the error will be thrown"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throwOnError"},{"type":"text","value":" is false (default when onError handler is provided), the error will be swallowed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both onError and throwOnError can be used together - the handler will be called before any error is thrown"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The error state can be checked using the underlying AsyncThrottler instance"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"state-management-and-selector","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State Management and Selector"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The hook uses TanStack Store for reactive state management. You can subscribe to state changes\nin two ways:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"throttler.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" component (Recommended for component tree subscriptions)"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" component to subscribe to state changes deep in your component tree without\nneeding to pass a selector to the hook. This is ideal when you want to subscribe to state\nin child components."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"selector"},{"type":"text","value":" parameter (For hook-level subscriptions)"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"selector"},{"type":"text","value":" parameter allows you to specify which state changes will trigger reactive updates\nat the hook level, optimizing performance by preventing unnecessary updates when irrelevant\nstate changes occur."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, there will be no reactive state subscriptions"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and you must opt-in to state\ntracking by providing a selector function or using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" component. This prevents unnecessary\nupdates and gives you full control over when your component tracks state changes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Available state properties:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"settleCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Number of function executions that have completed (success or error)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errorCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Number of function executions that have resulted in errors"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPending"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether the throttler is waiting for the timeout to trigger execution"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isExecuting"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether an async function execution is currently in progress"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastArgs"},{"type":"text","value":": The arguments from the most recent call to maybeExecute"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastExecutionTime"},{"type":"text","value":": Timestamp of the last execution"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lastResult"},{"type":"text","value":": The result from the most recent successful execution"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nextExecutionTime"},{"type":"text","value":": Timestamp of the next allowed execution"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":": Current execution status ('disabled' | 'idle' | 'pending' | 'executing' | 'settled')"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"unmount-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unmount behavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, the primitive cancels any pending execution and aborts any in-flight execution when the owning component unmounts.\nAbort only cancels underlying operations (e.g. fetch) when the abort signal from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getAbortSignal()"},{"type":"text","value":" is passed to them.\nUse the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUnmount"},{"type":"text","value":" option to customize this. For example, to flush pending work instead:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const throttler = createAsyncThrottler(fn, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  onUnmount: (t) => t.flush()\n});","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: For async utils, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a Promise and runs fire-and-forget in the cleanup.\nIf your throttled function updates Solid signals, those updates may run after the component has\nunmounted, which can cause unexpected reactive updates. Guard your callbacks accordingly when\nusing onUnmount with flush."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"type-parameters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Type Parameters"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"tfn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TFn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"extends"}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AnyAsyncFunction"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"tselected","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TSelected"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TSelected"},{"type":"text","value":" = {\n}"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"parameters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parameters"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"fn","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../type-aliases/SolidPacerOptions.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SolidPacerOptions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"link","href":"../interfaces/SolidAsyncThrottlerOptions.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SolidAsyncThrottlerOptions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TSelected"},{"type":"text","value":">>"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"selector","children":[{"type":"text","value":"selector?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":") => "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TSelected"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"returns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../interfaces/SolidAsyncThrottler.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SolidAsyncThrottler"}]},{"type":"text","value":"<"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TFn"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TSelected"},{"type":"text","value":">"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Default behavior - no reactive state subscriptions\nconst { maybeExecute } = createAsyncThrottler(\n  async (id: string) => {\n    const data = await api.fetchData(id);\n    return data;\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 }\n);\n\n// Opt-in to track isPending or isExecuting changes (optimized for loading states)\nconst throttler = createAsyncThrottler(\n  async (query) => {\n    const result = await searchAPI(query);\n    return result;\n  },\n  { wait: 2000 },\n  (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending, isExecuting: state.isExecuting })\n);\n\n// Opt-in to track error state changes (optimized for error handling)\nconst throttler = createAsyncThrottler(\n  async (query) => {\n    const result = await searchAPI(query);\n    return result;\n  },\n  {\n    wait: 2000,\n    leading: true,   // Execute immediately on first call\n    trailing: false, // Skip trailing edge updates\n    onError: (error) => {\n      console.error('API call failed:', error);\n    }\n  },\n  (state) => ({ errorCount: state.errorCount })\n);\n\n// Access the selected state (will be empty object {} unless selector provided)\nconst { isPending, isExecuting } = throttler.state();","lang":"tsx"}],"headings":[{"id":"state-management-and-selector","text":"State Management and Selector","level":2},{"id":"unmount-behavior","text":"Unmount behavior","level":2},{"id":"type-parameters","text":"Type Parameters","level":2},{"id":"tfn","text":"TFn","level":3},{"id":"tselected","text":"TSelected","level":3},{"id":"parameters","text":"Parameters","level":2},{"id":"fn","text":"fn","level":3},{"id":"options","text":"options","level":3},{"id":"selector","text":"selector?","level":3},{"id":"returns","text":"Returns","level":2},{"id":"example","text":"Example","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n```ts\nfunction createAsyncThrottler<TFn, TSelected>(\n   fn,\n   options,\nselector?): SolidAsyncThrottler<TFn, TSelected>;\n```\n\nDefined in: [async-throttler/createAsyncThrottler.ts:180](https://github.com/TanStack/pacer/blob/main/packages/solid-pacer/src/async-throttler/createAsyncThrottler.ts#L180)\n\nA low-level Solid hook that creates an `AsyncThrottler` instance to limit how often an async function can execute.\n\nThis hook is designed to be flexible and state-management agnostic - it simply returns a throttler instance that\nyou can integrate with any state management solution (createSignal, etc).\n\nAsync throttling ensures an async function executes at most once within a specified time window,\nregardless of how many times it is called. This is useful for rate-limiting expensive API calls,\ndatabase operations, or other async tasks.\n\nUnlike the non-async Throttler, this async version supports returning values from the throttled function,\nmaking it ideal for API calls and other async operations where you want the result of the `maybeExecute` call\ninstead of setting the result on a state variable from within the throttled function.\n\nError Handling:\n- If an `onError` handler is provided, it will be called with the error and throttler instance\n- If `throwOnError` is true (default when no onError handler is provided), the error will be thrown\n- If `throwOnError` is false (default when onError handler is provided), the error will be swallowed\n- Both onError and throwOnError can be used together - the handler will be called before any error is thrown\n- The error state can be checked using the underlying AsyncThrottler instance\n\n## State Management and Selector\n\nThe hook uses TanStack Store for reactive state management. You can subscribe to state changes\nin two ways:\n\n**1. Using `throttler.Subscribe` component (Recommended for component tree subscriptions)**\n\nUse the `Subscribe` component to subscribe to state changes deep in your component tree without\nneeding to pass a selector to the hook. This is ideal when you want to subscribe to state\nin child components.\n\n**2. Using the `selector` parameter (For hook-level subscriptions)**\n\nThe `selector` parameter allows you to specify which state changes will trigger reactive updates\nat the hook level, optimizing performance by preventing unnecessary updates when irrelevant\nstate changes occur.\n\n**By default, there will be no reactive state subscriptions** and you must opt-in to state\ntracking by providing a selector function or using the `Subscribe` component. This prevents unnecessary\nupdates and gives you full control over when your component tracks state changes.\n\nAvailable state properties:\n- `settleCount`: Number of function executions that have completed (success or error)\n- `errorCount`: Number of function executions that have resulted in errors\n- `isPending`: Whether the throttler is waiting for the timeout to trigger execution\n- `isExecuting`: Whether an async function execution is currently in progress\n- `lastArgs`: The arguments from the most recent call to maybeExecute\n- `lastExecutionTime`: Timestamp of the last execution\n- `lastResult`: The result from the most recent successful execution\n- `nextExecutionTime`: Timestamp of the next allowed execution\n- `status`: Current execution status ('disabled' | 'idle' | 'pending' | 'executing' | 'settled')\n\n## Unmount behavior\n\nBy default, the primitive cancels any pending execution and aborts any in-flight execution when the owning component unmounts.\nAbort only cancels underlying operations (e.g. fetch) when the abort signal from `getAbortSignal()` is passed to them.\nUse the `onUnmount` option to customize this. For example, to flush pending work instead:\n\n```tsx\nconst throttler = createAsyncThrottler(fn, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  onUnmount: (t) => t.flush()\n});\n```\n\nNote: For async utils, `flush()` returns a Promise and runs fire-and-forget in the cleanup.\nIf your throttled function updates Solid signals, those updates may run after the component has\nunmounted, which can cause unexpected reactive updates. Guard your callbacks accordingly when\nusing onUnmount with flush.\n\n## Type Parameters\n\n### TFn\n\n`TFn` *extends* `AnyAsyncFunction`\n\n### TSelected\n\n`TSelected` = \\{\n\\}\n\n## Parameters\n\n### fn\n\n`TFn`\n\n### options\n\n[`SolidPacerOptions`](../type-aliases/SolidPacerOptions.md)\\<[`SolidAsyncThrottlerOptions`](../interfaces/SolidAsyncThrottlerOptions.md)\\<`TFn`, `TSelected`\\>\\>\n\n### selector?\n\n(`state`) => `TSelected`\n\n## Returns\n\n[`SolidAsyncThrottler`](../interfaces/SolidAsyncThrottler.md)\\<`TFn`, `TSelected`\\>\n\n## Example\n\n```tsx\n// Default behavior - no reactive state subscriptions\nconst { maybeExecute } = createAsyncThrottler(\n  async (id: string) => {\n    const data = await api.fetchData(id);\n    return data;\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 }\n);\n\n// Opt-in to track isPending or isExecuting changes (optimized for loading states)\nconst throttler = createAsyncThrottler(\n  async (query) => {\n    const result = await searchAPI(query);\n    return result;\n  },\n  { wait: 2000 },\n  (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending, isExecuting: state.isExecuting })\n);\n\n// Opt-in to track error state changes (optimized for error handling)\nconst throttler = createAsyncThrottler(\n  async (query) => {\n    const result = await searchAPI(query);\n    return result;\n  },\n  {\n    wait: 2000,\n    leading: true,   // Execute immediately on first call\n    trailing: false, // Skip trailing edge updates\n    onError: (error) => {\n      console.error('API call failed:', error);\n    }\n  },\n  (state) => ({ errorCount: state.errorCount })\n);\n\n// Access the selected state (will be empty object {} unless selector provided)\nconst { isPending, isExecuting } = throttler.state();\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: createAsyncThrottler\ntitle: createAsyncThrottler\n---\n\n```ts\nfunction createAsyncThrottler<TFn, TSelected>(\n   fn,\n   options,\nselector?): SolidAsyncThrottler<TFn, TSelected>;\n```\n\nDefined in: [async-throttler/createAsyncThrottler.ts:180](https://github.com/TanStack/pacer/blob/main/packages/solid-pacer/src/async-throttler/createAsyncThrottler.ts#L180)\n\nA low-level Solid hook that creates an `AsyncThrottler` instance to limit how often an async function can execute.\n\nThis hook is designed to be flexible and state-management agnostic - it simply returns a throttler instance that\nyou can integrate with any state management solution (createSignal, etc).\n\nAsync throttling ensures an async function executes at most once within a specified time window,\nregardless of how many times it is called. This is useful for rate-limiting expensive API calls,\ndatabase operations, or other async tasks.\n\nUnlike the non-async Throttler, this async version supports returning values from the throttled function,\nmaking it ideal for API calls and other async operations where you want the result of the `maybeExecute` call\ninstead of setting the result on a state variable from within the throttled function.\n\nError Handling:\n- If an `onError` handler is provided, it will be called with the error and throttler instance\n- If `throwOnError` is true (default when no onError handler is provided), the error will be thrown\n- If `throwOnError` is false (default when onError handler is provided), the error will be swallowed\n- Both onError and throwOnError can be used together - the handler will be called before any error is thrown\n- The error state can be checked using the underlying AsyncThrottler instance\n\n## State Management and Selector\n\nThe hook uses TanStack Store for reactive state management. You can subscribe to state changes\nin two ways:\n\n**1. Using `throttler.Subscribe` component (Recommended for component tree subscriptions)**\n\nUse the `Subscribe` component to subscribe to state changes deep in your component tree without\nneeding to pass a selector to the hook. This is ideal when you want to subscribe to state\nin child components.\n\n**2. Using the `selector` parameter (For hook-level subscriptions)**\n\nThe `selector` parameter allows you to specify which state changes will trigger reactive updates\nat the hook level, optimizing performance by preventing unnecessary updates when irrelevant\nstate changes occur.\n\n**By default, there will be no reactive state subscriptions** and you must opt-in to state\ntracking by providing a selector function or using the `Subscribe` component. This prevents unnecessary\nupdates and gives you full control over when your component tracks state changes.\n\nAvailable state properties:\n- `settleCount`: Number of function executions that have completed (success or error)\n- `errorCount`: Number of function executions that have resulted in errors\n- `isPending`: Whether the throttler is waiting for the timeout to trigger execution\n- `isExecuting`: Whether an async function execution is currently in progress\n- `lastArgs`: The arguments from the most recent call to maybeExecute\n- `lastExecutionTime`: Timestamp of the last execution\n- `lastResult`: The result from the most recent successful execution\n- `nextExecutionTime`: Timestamp of the next allowed execution\n- `status`: Current execution status ('disabled' | 'idle' | 'pending' | 'executing' | 'settled')\n\n## Unmount behavior\n\nBy default, the primitive cancels any pending execution and aborts any in-flight execution when the owning component unmounts.\nAbort only cancels underlying operations (e.g. fetch) when the abort signal from `getAbortSignal()` is passed to them.\nUse the `onUnmount` option to customize this. For example, to flush pending work instead:\n\n```tsx\nconst throttler = createAsyncThrottler(fn, {\n  wait: 1000,\n  onUnmount: (t) => t.flush()\n});\n```\n\nNote: For async utils, `flush()` returns a Promise and runs fire-and-forget in the cleanup.\nIf your throttled function updates Solid signals, those updates may run after the component has\nunmounted, which can cause unexpected reactive updates. Guard your callbacks accordingly when\nusing onUnmount with flush.\n\n## Type Parameters\n\n### TFn\n\n`TFn` *extends* `AnyAsyncFunction`\n\n### TSelected\n\n`TSelected` = \\{\n\\}\n\n## Parameters\n\n### fn\n\n`TFn`\n\n### options\n\n[`SolidPacerOptions`](../type-aliases/SolidPacerOptions.md)\\<[`SolidAsyncThrottlerOptions`](../interfaces/SolidAsyncThrottlerOptions.md)\\<`TFn`, `TSelected`\\>\\>\n\n### selector?\n\n(`state`) => `TSelected`\n\n## Returns\n\n[`SolidAsyncThrottler`](../interfaces/SolidAsyncThrottler.md)\\<`TFn`, `TSelected`\\>\n\n## Example\n\n```tsx\n// Default behavior - no reactive state subscriptions\nconst { maybeExecute } = createAsyncThrottler(\n  async (id: string) => {\n    const data = await api.fetchData(id);\n    return data;\n  },\n  { wait: 1000 }\n);\n\n// Opt-in to track isPending or isExecuting changes (optimized for loading states)\nconst throttler = createAsyncThrottler(\n  async (query) => {\n    const result = await searchAPI(query);\n    return result;\n  },\n  { wait: 2000 },\n  (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending, isExecuting: state.isExecuting })\n);\n\n// Opt-in to track error state changes (optimized for error handling)\nconst throttler = createAsyncThrottler(\n  async (query) => {\n    const result = await searchAPI(query);\n    return result;\n  },\n  {\n    wait: 2000,\n    leading: true,   // Execute immediately on first call\n    trailing: false, // Skip trailing edge updates\n    onError: (error) => {\n      console.error('API call failed:', error);\n    }\n  },\n  (state) => ({ errorCount: state.errorCount })\n);\n\n// Access the selected state (will be empty object {} unless selector provided)\nconst { isPending, isExecuting } = throttler.state();\n```\n"}
